From 9c0e4377b70688ce8d427b182c5572bcc34e89a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:22:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 06/12] scheduler: count what happens, send totals once a
 second, end expired streams

A dashboard shows totals and rates: builds per minute, the cache hit
rate, bytes published. Summing events cannot give them, because a
watcher sees only what happened while it was connected. So the
Dispatcher keeps counters for its current term as leader: new entries,
cache hits and misses, assignments, outcomes, requeues, bytes
published, and an estimate of the bytes fetched from the cache.

The counters run whether anyone watches or not, so with nobody watching
a cancel or a leaving client counts the entries it retired instead of
the events it would have sent. The tests run the same farm watched and
unwatched and compare the totals. dispatcher-bench firehose shows no
measurable cost.

Stats goes out once a second while anyone watches, with the totals and
the per-system numbers of the nix_grpc_sched_system metric. It doubles
as the heartbeat. Reset carries a sample of the counters for each of
the last 60 minutes, so a client has "last N minutes" totals as soon as
it connects. Counters and history start over when the node becomes the
leader again, while instance and seq continue.

A Watch stream can stay open for days, past its bearer token. A stream
opened with one now ends with UNAUTHENTICATED ("bearer token expired")
once the scheduler would refuse the token, a minute after its exp. The
reactor checks with each event, and Stats makes that at most a second
late. Streams of certificate callers do not end this way.

events-grpc-test also covers viewers that a trusted proxy names in
x-forwarded-client-cert: the access rules and the 8-stream cap apply to
the forwarded name, the proxy's own certificate stands in for nobody,
and two forwarded headers name nobody.
